# The buckets this store manages, one per line — COMMITTED EXAMPLE.
#
# The store reads bootstrap/buckets.conf, which is gitignored: the buckets a
# deployment carries are that deployment's business, the same way its .env is.
# `make` copies this file into place the first time buckets.conf is missing.
# Declare YOUR buckets in buckets.conf; edit this file only to change the
# example everyone starts from.
#
# Format:
#
#   <bucket>  <noncurrent-version expiry in days>  <class>  [extra consumers]
#
# This file is the single source of truth. The bootstrap creates from it, the
# credential helper generates from it, the smoke test asserts against it.
#
# ── Classes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   published   Gets a PUBLISHER key (put/get/list, no delete) and a CONSUMER
#               key (get/list). Data that is meant to be read.
#
#   restricted  Gets an INGEST key only (put/get/list, no delete). NO consumer
#               key exists, so there is no credential anywhere that a reader
#               could be handed. For raw or sensitive source data.
#
# The class is not decoration. It is the difference between "the raw files are
# on the server" and "the raw files are readable by everyone with a key".
#
# ── Extra consumers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The optional fourth column is a comma-separated list of additional readers of
# a published bucket. Each gets its OWN key on the same read-only policy, so a
# compromised or retired reader is revoked on its own rather than by rotating a
# key every reader shares. Names must be lowercase [a-z0-9_].
#
#   example-publish  730  published  reporting,notebooks
#
# gives EXAMPLE_PUBLISH_CONSUMER_REPORTING_KEY / _SECRET and
# EXAMPLE_PUBLISH_CONSUMER_NOTEBOOKS_KEY / _SECRET alongside the default
# EXAMPLE_PUBLISH_CONSUMER_KEY / _SECRET.
#
# ── Retention ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The expiry column applies to NON-CURRENT object versions only — the history
# left behind when a write overwrites an object. Current objects are never
# expired automatically, because silently deleting data someone is mid-analysis
# on is worse than keeping it. Deleting current data is a deliberate operator
# action.
#
# ── Adding a bucket ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   1. add a line to bootstrap/buckets.conf   (not to this .example file)
#   2. make secrets     (appends and fills the credentials it needs)
#   3. make bootstrap
#   4. make test
#
# Renaming or removing a line does NOT delete anything. The bootstrap's drift
# report names what is left over; retire it by hand with the root credentials.

# bucket           days  class       extra consumers
example-publish    730   published
example-raw        730   restricted
